---
/* The two filters every illustration on the site shares, defined once per
   page. They are what makes hand-authored SVG read as drawn rather than as a
   stroke icon: a turbulence field displaces the path, so a mathematically
   clean curve picks up a wobble no coordinate anyone could type would have.

   This is the same technique --grain already uses (tokens.css), so it is a
   known quantity here: an inline SVG filter, no request, no dependency.

   Two strengths, because the ink line and the flat colour behind it want
   different amounts of roughness — the line should look drawn, the flat shape
   should look printed slightly wrong.

   Render this ONCE per page, above the illustrations that reference it. The
   ids are global, which is the point: five <Illustration> instances share one
   filter rather than defining five. */
---

<svg class="ink-defs" width="0" height="0" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
  <defs>
    <filter id="ink-line" x="-20%" y="-20%" width="140%" height="140%">
      <feTurbulence
        type="fractalNoise"
        baseFrequency="0.028"
        numOctaves="4"
        seed="3"
        result="n"
      />
      <feDisplacementMap
        in="SourceGraphic"
        in2="n"
        scale="4"
        xChannelSelector="R"
        yChannelSelector="G"
      />
    </filter>

    <filter id="ink-flat" x="-25%" y="-25%" width="150%" height="150%">
      <feTurbulence
        type="fractalNoise"
        baseFrequency="0.018"
        numOctaves="3"
        seed="5"
        result="n"
      />
      <feDisplacementMap
        in="SourceGraphic"
        in2="n"
        scale="6"
        xChannelSelector="R"
        yChannelSelector="G"
      />
    </filter>
  </defs>
</svg>

<style>
  /* Out of flow entirely — it draws nothing itself. width/height 0 is not
     enough on its own in every engine, so it is also positioned away. */
  .ink-defs {
    position: absolute;
    inline-size: 0;
    block-size: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
</style>
